1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,356
The Sun has just vanished.

2
00:00:02,356 --> 00:00:07,178
And for eight minutes and twenty
seconds, you will not notice a thing.

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:14,361
Not exploded. Not burned out. Simply gone.
This is what happens next, minute by minute.

4
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:19,810
For now, nothing changes.

5
00:00:19,810 --> 00:00:30,858
The Sun is still up there in the sky, shining. You are looking at light that had
already left it, and that light takes eight minutes and twenty seconds to cross the gap.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,099
Stranger still, the Earth keeps
circling a star that is no longer there.

7
00:00:37,099 --> 00:00:47,302
Gravity is not instant. Changes in it travel at the speed of light,
too. So for eight minutes, our planet orbits an empty point in space.

8
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,441
Eight minutes, twenty seconds.

9
00:00:51,441 --> 00:00:56,986
The last light arrives. And the whole
day side of the Earth goes dark at once.

10
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:02,677
At the same moment, the Sun's pull lets go.

11
00:01:02,677 --> 00:01:11,347
The Earth stops curving, and leaves in a straight line, at
about thirty kilometres every second. The Moon comes with us.

12
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:16,271
But the Moon only ever
shone by reflected sunlight.

13
00:01:16,271 --> 00:01:19,464
Within about two seconds, it fades out.

14
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,293
The planets shine the same way.

15
00:01:23,293 --> 00:01:30,450
But they are far away, and their last reflected
light is still travelling towards us. So they linger.

16
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:34,671
Venus and Mars go out
within about half an hour.

17
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:40,230
Jupiter keeps shining for more than
an hour. Saturn, for more than two.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,905
Then, only the stars are left.

19
00:01:43,905 --> 00:01:48,658
They are suns in their own right.
And they haven't noticed a thing.

20
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:53,871
On the ground, photosynthesis stops.

21
00:01:53,871 --> 00:02:09,296
Every plant on Earth, and the plankton in the sea, has lost its only source of energy. Small plants die
within weeks. Large trees, living on stored sugar, might last for years, if they didn't freeze first.

22
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,524
Because the cold comes quickly.

23
00:02:12,524 --> 00:02:17,463
With nothing to replace it, the
planet's heat leaks away into space.

24
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:20,065
These are rough estimates.

25
00:02:20,065 --> 00:02:35,927
But within about a week, the average surface temperature falls below freezing. The most quoted figure is around
minus eighteen degrees Celsius. Within a year, around minus seventy. Colder than a winter at the South Pole.

26
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:42,495
A few people might hold out, gathered
around heat that never came from the Sun:

27
00:02:42,495 --> 00:02:46,514
nuclear reactors, and the
warmth of the Earth itself.

28
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:51,491
The oceans freeze over, from the top down.

29
00:02:51,491 --> 00:03:01,747
But ice floats, and it insulates. Beneath that lid, kilometres of
water stay liquid. By one estimate, for hundreds of thousands of years.

30
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,211
And down there, in the dark,
some life would barely notice.

31
00:03:07,211 --> 00:03:19,275
Around hydrothermal vents, microbes live on chemical energy rising from inside the
planet. Of everything alive on Earth, they have the best chance of carrying on.

32
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,206
Above the ice, it keeps getting colder.

33
00:03:24,206 --> 00:03:31,714
Eventually, and here the estimates run from decades
to far longer, the air itself begins to condense.

34
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:39,232
At normal pressure, oxygen turns liquid at
minus one hundred and eighty-three degrees.

35
00:03:39,232 --> 00:03:46,933
Nitrogen follows, at minus one hundred and
ninety-six. The sky falls as rain. Then, as snow.

36
00:03:48,750 --> 00:04:00,142
Millions of years on, the Earth settles near minus two hundred and forty degrees,
held just above absolute zero by the slow leak of heat from its own interior.

37
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:09,423
And so the Earth drifts on. A dark, frozen
world. One more wanderer among the stars.

38
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,193
None of this is going to happen.

39
00:04:15,193 --> 00:04:22,411
A star cannot simply disappear. The laws of physics
do not let that much matter and energy vanish.

40
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:30,197
Our Sun is about halfway through its life, with
roughly five billion years of steady light still ahead.

41
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:34,709
So tomorrow morning,
when it rises, remember:

42
00:04:34,709 --> 00:04:40,009
that light is eight minutes old. And
everything alive here depends on it.

